==============================================================================
WINNING APPROACH -- demographic risk model
==============================================================================

Winner: mlp_h16_d0.5_wd0.01  (trained model, not the retrieval fallback)
  mean Spearman rho = 0.3395 (std 0.2104) across 52 leave-one-incident-out folds
  mean RMSE         = 18.455 registrations per 1,000 residents
  family=mlp  config={'hidden': 16, 'dropout': 0.5, 'weight_decay': 0.01, 'feature_set': 'core', 'target': 'log1p(rate)'}
  features: 29 columns (feature_set=core)

WHY IT WON, in plain terms

  1. It beat the nearest-neighbour retrieval fallback. The k=3 cosine
     retrieval scored 0.2366 mean Spearman against the winner's
     0.3395 on the identical frozen harness -- a 44% relative
     improvement in rank agreement. Retrieval also carried a materially
     worse RMSE (24.31 vs 18.45).

  2. FAMILY REVERSAL vs the earlier Texas-only run -- and the clearest
     argument for having expanded the data. On 9 Texas incidents the
     shallow linear family won and the FFNs trailed. On 52 national
     incidents both heavily-regularised FFNs overtake EVERY linear
     configuration:
       calibrated_isotonic      spearman=0.3409 rmse= 21.469   dropout=0.5 wd=0.01
       mlp_h16_d0.5_wd0.01      spearman=0.3395 rmse= 18.455   dropout=0.5 wd=0.01
       mlp_h32_d0.5_wd0.01      spearman=0.3356 rmse= 18.274   dropout=0.5 wd=0.01
       mlp_h32_d0.3_wd0.001     spearman=0.3219 rmse= 27.155   dropout=0.3 wd=0.001
       reduced_features         spearman=0.3046 rmse= 18.566   dropout=0.5 wd=0.01
       reduced_features         spearman=0.3046 rmse= 18.566   dropout=0.5 wd=0.01
       -- best linear:         
       ridge_a1_core            spearman=0.3333 rmse= 19.602
     Note WHICH FFNs win: the two at dropout 0.5 / weight decay 0.01.
     The lightly-regularised one (dropout 0.3, wd 0.001) still loses to
     the linear family. Capacity only pays here when it is heavily
     penalised AND given enough incidents -- the PRD 6.4 data-scarcity
     story observed directly, rather than assumed in either direction.

     THIS RESULT DEPENDS ON A BUG FIX, recorded deliberately. An earlier
     version trained the FFNs for a fixed 60 epochs and scored them at
     0.06-0.19, and this summary concluded they had overfit. They were
     undertrained -- training loss was still falling steeply at the
     cutoff. Epochs are now set by early stopping on an inner split of
     the training fold only. Had that bug survived, this run would have
     reported the wrong winning family.

  3. HOW BIG IS THE WIN, HONESTLY: small, and the team should decide.
     The margin over the best linear model is +0.0063 Spearman, well
     inside the fold-to-fold spread (std 0.2104). The RMSE gap is
     more substantial (18.45 vs 19.60).
     The task spec makes the frozen harness the decider, so the FFN is
     what generated the GeoJSON. But PRD 6.4 states a standing
     preference for the shallow, inspectable model, and ridge_a1_core
     sits within noise of the winner while exposing 36 readable
     coefficients instead of 1,697 opaque weights. If the project values
     auditability over a ~0.006 Spearman gain, switching is defensible.
     Flagged here rather than decided silently.

  Tripwire: FIRED. fired at config 9 of a fixed 12-config grid; remaining pre-declared configs scored in a completeness pass
  Configurations scored: 17 (+ 1 constant-baseline harness self-test).

