==============================================================================
LIMITATIONS -- carry these into any UI label (addendum Section 6)
==============================================================================

  * TRACT RESOLUTION, FOOTPRINT DISPLAY. The model predicts at census
    tract resolution. Every per-building weight in demographic_risk.geojson
    is that single tract score split across footprints by building area,
    NOT a per-building prediction. Two adjacent buildings differ in weight
    only because they differ in footprint area. Do not read an individual
    building's weight as a statement about that building.

  * ACS COUNTS USUAL RESIDENTS, NOT TRANSIENT POPULATIONS. The ACS and
    SVI inputs describe where people usually live. They do not capture
    summer camp populations, RV parks, short-term rentals, or day
    visitors -- precisely the populations most exposed in the July 2025
    Kerr County event. This model therefore understates risk exactly
    where the Guadalupe River camp corridor sits.

  * SMALL-SAMPLE VALIDATION. 52 incidents means 52 folds. The
    fold-to-fold spread is wide (std 0.2104); a single
    unusual disaster moves the mean materially. Treat the headline score
    as an order-of-magnitude indication of skill, not a precise estimate.

  * THE LABEL IS SELF-REPORTED AID UPTAKE, NOT DAMAGE. OpenFEMA counts
    who registered for Individual Assistance. That reflects outreach
    intensity, awareness, and eligibility as much as physical flooding.
    Rank agreement (Spearman) is reported as primary for this reason.

  * WEAK AND NOT SIGNIFICANT WITHIN KERR -- THE MOST IMPORTANT CAVEAT.
    On the held-out DR-4879 fold the model reaches Spearman 0.406
    across all 528 declared tracts. But restricted to the 14 KERR
    COUNTY tracts the heatmap actually covers, rank correlation is
    +0.27 with p=0.36 -- on 14 points that is not
    distinguishable from chance, and it has swung sign between model
    versions (-0.11 under the earlier linear winner). Kerr's tracts are
    demographically similar and were all severely hit; what actually
    separated them was proximity to the Guadalupe River, which is
    hydrology the model never sees. Treat this layer as a coarse
    demographic-vulnerability backdrop. It must NOT be read as 'which
    parts of Kerr County flooded' -- that is the physics engine's job,
    and this layer carries no information about it.

  * MODEST ABSOLUTE SKILL. Mean Spearman ~0.34 is a real but weak
    signal. This output is a prioritisation hint, never an authority for
    who gets help. Per PRD Design Principle 3 it must be shown alongside
    the citation-grounded critique, never on its own.

  * VALIDATED SCOPE (adopted 2026-09-06; see
    logs/model_tests_followup.log Section 7 for the full statement).
    Measured like-for-like inside each fold, this model ranks WORSE in
    nonmetro tracts (Spearman 0.238, RUCC 4-9) than metro (0.347).
    Kerr County is RUCC 4, so THE DEPLOYMENT TARGET SITS IN THE MODEL'S
    WEAKER STRATUM. That gap survived a paired, size-matched audit
    holding the incident constant (p=0.019) and is stable across
    sample-size floors of 30/50/100 rural tracts per fold -- it is a
    real property of the model, not small-sample noise. Use for
    within-incident RELATIVE shading only; never as a magnitude, a
    cross-incident comparison, or a flood-extent claim.

  * CALIBRATION IS BROKEN AND WAS NOT REPAIRED (Test 2). Predictions
    run ~4x low, and the calibration curve is NON-MONOTONIC: decile 1
    carries the second-highest median outcome of any decile. An
    isotonic wrapper was fitted and REJECTED for making RMSE worse
    (18.455 -> 21.469). The worst misses are dense urban tracts
    (Harris County, DR-4781: under 1 per 1,000 predicted against an
    actual 186-265) -- a magnitude failure, not a ranking failure,
    consistent with a model that has no hydrological input.

  * ABSTENTION RULE IS ACTIVE (Test 6). Median imputation is unsafe
    here -- one missing feature drops rank correlation to 0.58, three
    to 0.11 -- so a tract is scored only with complete features
    (max_missing_features=0). Footprints failing this carry
    weight=null and score_source="insufficient_data" and MUST NOT be
    rendered as zero. Currently fires on 0 of 14 Kerr tracts.

  * A RURAL-UPWEIGHTING FIX WAS CONSIDERED AND DECLINED, not
    overlooked. Reweighting adds no information (it amplifies the
    3,160 rural rows already held), and the real-data lever is nearly
    exhausted in scope. Design retained, unexecuted, in
    logs/proposed_rural_experiment.log.

==============================================================================
SECTION 7 FLAGS -- items referred back rather than worked around
==============================================================================

  1. CENSUS API KEY GATE (blocking for api.census.gov only, worked
     around legitimately, not silently). Every api.census.gov query --
     including the credential-free example in the task spec -- now
     returns a 'Missing Key' interstitial. Rather than sign up for a
     key (a Section 7 stop condition), the identical ACS 5-year
     estimates were taken from the ungated table-based Summary Files on
     www2.census.gov, a host the spec's own network check requires.
     ACTION FOR THE USER: if you want the REST path, request a free key
     at api.census.gov/data/key_signup.html and set CENSUS_API_KEY;
     scripts/pull_acs.py already detects it.

  2. NOT ATTEMPTED, as instructed: camp footprints (KCAD / paid
     aggregator), AAR corpus PDFs, and all gated mobility data
     (Cuebiq / SafeGraph / Veraset). These remain separate manual tasks.

  3. The 'Demographic Risk Model + Weighted-Footprint Heatmap' addendum
     is not present in this repository. Section 4.3's property schema
     was taken from the inline definition in the task spec itself
     ({weight, feature_id, score_source, citation_ids}); the ACS/SVI
     variable pairing was chosen to span the standard SVI themes.
     Worth confirming against the addendum before the UI consumes this.

